%% ABSTRACT-BEGIN
\begin{abstract}
Optically pumped magnetometers (OPMs) can be placed on the scalp, where the magnetic field of cortical sources is stronger than at the superconducting quantum interference devices (SQUIDs) of conventional magnetoencephalography (MEG). For interictal epileptiform discharges, however, OPMs have shown a higher signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) than SQUIDs in some comparisons but not in others. A spherical-head analysis using point sensors at fixed standoffs and an assumed OPM-to-SQUID noise ratio predicted the largest on-scalp benefit for superficial sources and small heads. Using realistic head models, we examined how relative SNR depends on noise, array design and helmet fit. Under sensor, brain and environmental noise, we compared the matched-filter detectability of cortical sources between the << F.g2_squid_channels >>-channel Neuromag SQUID system and two single-axis OPM arrays, a dense array (<< F.g2_dense_sites >> sensors) and a site-matched array at the SQUID sites (<< F.g2_matched_sites >> sensors). We placed an adult head, << F.wr_n_scaled_words >> downscaled copies and << F.wr_n_principal_templates_words >> infant templates each in the adult helmet and in a helmet fitted at the adult's median sensor-to-scalp distance. In the adult, brain noise dominated the noise of both systems and reversed the ranking obtained under sensor noise alone. At an OPM noise of << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz, the dense array had higher detectability at almost all cortical sources (median ratio << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>, << F.g2_ci_level_pct >> parcel-bootstrap interval << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>), whereas the site-matched array showed no clear difference. OPM noise and array design determined which system had higher detectability; the SQUID channels compared and the SNR measure determined which system a sensor-level SNR favored. However, the dense-array advantage was as large as the noise model's underestimate of SQUID detectability. With measured SQUID noise and modeled OPM noise, the ratio fell to << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ci >>. In the adult helmet, the smaller heads had greater sensor-to-scalp distances and a larger dense-array advantage than the adult (by << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB). In the fitted helmet, with fewer dense-array sites on each smaller head, almost none of this excess remained (<< F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB). Under the modeled noise, the dense array detected simulated superficial spikes at a << F.cf_adult_principal_dense_vs_combined_practical_reduction_pct_range >> lower dipole strength than the SQUID system (adult, and smaller heads in the adult helmet), with idealized detectors at a nominal false-event rate. Overall, the results suggest that noise, array design and helmet fit, rather than sensor proximity alone, determine whether OPMs provide a higher SNR than SQUIDs, which is of interest when OPM and SQUID recordings are compared in pediatric and epilepsy studies.
\end{abstract}
