\section{Materials and methods}\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Study design and estimands}\label{sec:design}

To compare arrays without committing to a particular detector, we used the known-topography detectability of each source. This is the output SNR of a matched filter that knows the source's field pattern and the noise covariance,
\begin{equation}
d = \sqrt{\symbf{s}^{\top}\symbf{C}^{-1}\symbf{s}}.
\label{eq:d}
\end{equation}
Here, $\symbf{s}$ is the noise-free field of a << F.g2_focal_nam >>-nAm cortical dipole at the array's channels. We treated the array's noise covariance, $\symbf{C}$ (Section~\ref{sec:noise}), as known, using the true covariance of the modeled noise. We compared arrays target by target using the ratio $d_{\mathrm{OPM}}/d_{\mathrm{Neuromag}}$ or its value in decibels,
\begin{equation}
D = 20\log_{10}\frac{d_{\mathrm{OPM}}}{d_{\mathrm{Neuromag}}},
\label{eq:D}
\end{equation}
which is positive when the OPM array has higher detectability.

We summarized each comparison over the cortex, taking for the adult the unweighted median of target-wise ratios over all << F.g2_n_targets >> targets, including the << F.g2_medialwall_n >> medial-wall vertices. Because the smaller heads differ in cortical area, we summarized each by the area-weighted median of $D$ over its cortical targets, excluding the medial wall.

We set the adult's aggregation convention before adding the smaller heads and retained it rather than change it after the results were known. For the dense array, excluding the medial-wall vertices would change the adult's median ratio to << F.dp("wr_g2_dense_ib_ratio_no_medial_wall", 2) >>; the smaller heads' area-weighted convention would give << F.dp("wr_adult_dense_ib_ratio_area_weighted_cortical_3dp", 2) >> (<< F.g3b_link_g2_db >>~dB).

To compare a smaller head with the adult, we placed both in the same helmet and pose (Section~\ref{sec:arrays}). We computed the change from the adult, $\Delta$, from paired differences, rather than the difference of medians, with pairing determined by the anatomy. For the scaled adults, downscaled copies sharing the adult's vertices, we computed $\Delta$ as the median of $D_{\mathrm{head}}(v) - D_{\mathrm{adult}}(v)$ over common vertices, weighted by the smaller head's target areas. The other heads have their own cortical surfaces rather than the adult's vertices, so we first took the area-weighted median of $D$ within each Desikan--Killiany atlas parcel containing at least << F.g3b_cfg_min_n >> targets in both heads. In the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month templates, << F.g3b_infant2yr_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_n >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_n >> parcels, respectively, met this criterion. We then computed $\Delta$ as the median of the parcel differences, weighted by the adult's parcel areas.

\subsection{Head models}\label{sec:heads}

We used the MNE sample subject as the adult head \citep{gramfort2013}, with its FreeSurfer surfaces and atlas labels \citep{desikan2006,fischl2012}. Targets were vertices of an MNE-Python oct-6 source space on the white-matter surface, each modeled as a << F.g2_focal_nam >>-nAm dipole normal to the cortex. We retained only vertices at least << F.g2_source_skull_clearance_mm >>~mm inside the inner skull and defined target depth as the distance to the nearest MRI scalp point. We computed forward fields with a three-layer boundary-element model (BEM; \citealp{hamalainen1989}), with conductivities of << F.g2_conductivity >>~S/m and the MRI scalp as its outer surface. We solved the BEM by linear collocation, using << F.g2_head_triangles_coarse >> triangles per skull surface and a scalp refined to << F.g2_head_triangles >> triangles.

We checked the BEM against an exact three-shell sphere and by refining the scalp. Relative to the exact sphere's field, the BEM field had a 95th-percentile error of << F.g2_bemsphere_20480_h2mm_p95_pct >> at 2~mm and << F.g2_bemsphere_20480_h15mm_p95_pct >> at 15~mm outside the outer surface. On a subset of targets, refining the scalp from << F.g2_head_triangles_coarse >> to << F.g2_head_triangles >> triangles changed the dense-array ratio from << F.g2_skin_bem3_5120_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> to << F.g2_skin_bem3_skin20480_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >>.

Four of the << F.wr_n_smaller_heads_words >> smaller heads form the basis of the main analysis (Table~\ref{tab:anatomies}); we call them the principal smaller heads. << F.wr_n_scaled_words|capitalize >> were made by scaling the adult, one to school-age size and the other to the head circumference of the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template. These scaled adults preserve the adult anatomy exactly and are therefore the main test of head size and helmet fit. The other << F.wr_n_principal_templates_words >> are the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month infant templates, averages of many MRIs \citep{oreilly2021,richards2016}, which add infant head shapes to the analysis, subject to the scalp-convention caveat below. None of the anatomies is an independent replication, because all share the noise calibration, the adult's conductivities and the processing.

The remaining << F.wr_n_provisional_words >> heads are reported separately in Section~S10. An MRI check classed the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template as << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_verdict >> because its white surface fitted its T1-weighted image best after a << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_best_shift_mm >>-mm shift, compared with << F.wr_qc_templates_other_white_best_shift_mm >>~mm in the other two templates. We obtained MRIs of << F.wr_n_children_words >> typically developing school-aged children, A, B and C (<< F.g3b_children_age_range >> years), from OpenNeuro dataset ds005234 \citep{fadeev2024,fadeev2025}.

We prepared the children's anatomy in three further steps (Section~S4). In this dataset, their inner skull lay a median of << F.g3b_children_watershed_inner_skull_depth_p50_range >>~mm below the scalp over the upper head. We therefore displaced it inward toward << F.g3b_cfg_child_skull_depth >>~mm below the scalp, keeping it at least << F.g3b_cfg_child_skull_min_cortex >>~mm from the white-surface vertices. We then transferred the adult's fiducials to the children by a similarity transform fitted between the white surfaces and checked the children's surfaces against their T1-weighted MRIs (Fig.~S6). The check found the surfaces registered to the images, but it cannot verify cortex within a few millimeters of the scalp. The << F.g2_source_skull_clearance_mm >>-mm target limit also excluded << F.wr_excl_children_lt8mm_pct_range >> of the children's cortex within 8~mm of the scalp, so their results do not describe a near-scalp cortex.

Further checks of the principal heads concerned scalp conventions and conductivity layers. The infant templates' scalps lie << F.wr_qc_principal_templates_offset_outside_otsu_mm_range >>~mm outside their MRI head boundaries, whereas the adult's scalp lies << F.wr_qc_adult_offset_inside_otsu_mm >>~mm inside its boundary. The difference between the two conventions is << F.wr_qc_principal_template_adult_convention_mm_range >>~mm (Section~S4.3). We also replaced the three-layer BEM with a single-compartment BEM, which has no skull or scalp conductivity layer. This changed the difference between each principal head's median $D$ and the adult's by << F.meth_principal_bem1_change_db_range >>~dB. This check tests the conductivity layers, not the position of the scalp or inner skull.

