\subsection{Sensor arrays and helmets}\label{sec:arrays}

We modeled the Neuromag system (<< F.g2_squid_channels >> channels; ``Neuromag'' below) using the sample recording's sensor geometry and MNE-Python's T3 coil definitions (types << F.meth_coil_grad_type >> and << F.meth_coil_mag_type >>). We integrated each gradiometer at << F.meth_coil_grad_points >> points and each magnetometer at << F.meth_coil_mag_points >>. We set Neuromag's white noise to the typical TRIUX datasheet values: << F.meth_squid_mag_asd >>~fT/√Hz for magnetometers and << F.meth_squid_grad_asd >>~fT/(cm~√Hz) for gradiometers \citep{elekta_triux}. The coils were << F.meth_dewar_mm >>~mm outward from the helmet's inner surface (the Dewar spacing), and no signal-space separation was applied. We compared each OPM array with all << F.g2_squid_channels >> Neuromag channels and, as alternative comparators, with the << F.g2_squid_grad_channels >> gradiometers or << F.g2_squid_sites >> magnetometers alone. Besides detectability, we computed peak-channel SNR and mean-power SNR \citep{goldenholz2009}, the sensor-level measures defined in Table~\ref{tab:estimands}.

We modeled each OPM as a single-axis sensor with its sensing center nominally << F.g2_dense_standoff_mm >>~mm above the MRI scalp \citep{alem2023,osborne2018}. We displaced sites outward if their sensor cells would otherwise have come within << F.g2_cell_clearance_rule_mm >>~mm of the head surface. In the adult, this affected << F.g2_dense_moved_out >> of the << F.g2_dense_sites >> dense sites and << F.g2_matched_moved_out >> of the << F.g2_matched_sites >> site-matched sites. We averaged each OPM's field over a << F.g2_opm_cell_mm >>-mm cube with a << F.meth_opm_cell_points >>-point Gauss--Legendre rule, a modeling choice that changes the peak field by at most << F.wr_opm_cell_peak_effect_pct >>. The sensitive axis was the head-surface normal, averaged within << F.meth_opm_axis_radius_mm >>~mm of the site. Had nearly radial axes been used instead, the dense-array ratio after projecting out the room field would have been << F.dp("meth_hse_radial_dense_proj_ratio_3dp", 2) >> rather than << F.dp("meth_hse_final_dense_proj_ratio_3dp", 2) >> (Sections~\ref{sec:noise} and S3).

We set OPM white noise, flat over << F.g2_band_range_hz >>~Hz, to << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz in the primary analysis and varied it over << F.wr_opm_asd_sweep_list >>~fT/√Hz. The primary level was a declared value rather than a device specification (Section~S8.3).

Both OPM arrays were restricted to the same scalp region, above the plane through the preauricular points and a point << F.wr_cover_brow_offset_mm >>~mm above the nasion. No OPM was within << F.g2_preauricular_exclusion_mm >>~mm of either preauricular point. Packing the dense array at a minimum spacing of << F.g2_dense_packing_mm >>~mm gave << F.g2_dense_sites >> sites on the adult, more than any clinical OPM array in Table~S19. For the site-matched array, we placed one OPM at each Neuromag site whose scalp projection fell within this region: << F.g2_matched_sites >> of << F.g2_squid_sites >> sites (Fig.~\ref{fig:arrays}). Clinical systems reach about 100--128 channels with dual- or triaxial sensors at fewer sites \citep{feys2025,shen2026}. We did not model these arrays. Neither single-axis array represents them, because both differ from clinical arrays in site count and sensing axes. To test the effect of extra axes, however, we added a triaxial array at the site-matched sites (<< F.g2_triax_channels >> channels).

In the fixed adult helmet, we used the adult's sensors and noise unchanged for every head (Fig.~\ref{fig:helmets}). For the primary placement, we positioned each head's coordinate frame where the adult's had been recorded, then raised the head until the nearest magnetometer coil was << F.g3b_cfg_clearance >>~mm from the scalp. We call this placement top contact, a best-fit assumption that favors Neuromag \citep{gaetz2008,tesan2010}. To describe the effect of placement, we also used << F.g3b_n_family_placements >> source-blind placements chosen from the geometry alone (contacts, shifts and rotations; Section~S5). In contrast, we rebuilt the OPM arrays on every smaller head, using the same coverage and spacing rules for the dense array. The site-matched array used that head's Neuromag sites at top contact (Table~\ref{tab:anatomies}).

To separate head size from helmet fit, we also placed each head in a helmet fitted at the adult's gap. We translated the head laterally from the recorded pose to the helmet midline without raising it to contact (the laterally centered pose). We then scaled the helmet about the head origin until the median magnetometer-to-scalp gap matched the adult's in the same pose, << F.rev_cgap_adult_gap_mm >>~mm. For comparison, the adult's gap is << F.g2_squid_scalp_dist_median_mm >>~mm at its measured head position and << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_gap_mm >>~mm at top contact. No coil could come within the << F.g3b_cfg_dewar_spacing >>-mm Dewar spacing of the scalp; no principal head reached this limit. The fitted helmet retains the adult's sensors and therefore does not model a pediatric SQUID system.

Our primary measure of helmet fit is the paired change, from the adult to a smaller head, in how much detectability Neuromag loses in the fixed helmet relative to the helmet fitted at the adult's gap. We call this measure the interaction. For each target, the contrast between helmets within one head is
\begin{equation}
F(v) = D_{\mathrm{fixed}}(v) - D_{\mathrm{fitted}}(v) = 20\log_{10}\frac{d_{\mathrm{Neuromag,\,fitted}}(v)}{d_{\mathrm{Neuromag,\,fixed}}(v)},
\label{eq:F}
\end{equation}
in which OPM detectability cancels. Because the fitted helmet is built about the laterally centered pose rather than at contact, the contrast $F$ combines changes in helmet scale and pose. We obtained the interaction for a smaller head by applying the $\Delta$ estimator of Section~\ref{sec:design} to $F$ instead of $D$. For the scaled adults, this is the median of $F_{\mathrm{head}}(v) - F_{\mathrm{adult}}(v)$ over common vertices, weighted by the smaller head's target areas. For the templates, it is the median of parcel differences, weighted by the adult's parcel areas.

As a median of paired differences, the interaction equals neither the difference of the two median contrasts nor $\Delta_{\mathrm{fixed}} - \Delta_{\mathrm{fitted}}$. Table~S6 gives each head's non-additivity residual. The interaction does not depend on the assumed OPM noise, because the detectability of the OPM array, which is the same in both helmets, cancels in $F$. It does, however, depend on the modeled Neuromag noise and on the anatomy.

We used three secondary controls of the construction (Section~S5). We fitted one helmet at the adult's top-contact gap, << F.rev_cgap_adult_top_gap_mm >>~mm, and scaled another with the head, thereby scaling the gap as well. To compare heads at equal OPM site count, we kept the helmet fitted at the adult's gap unchanged and subsampled the adult's dense array to each smaller head's site count.

