\subsection{Noise model, validation and sensitivity analyses}\label{sec:noise}

We exposed all arrays to the same noise sources in a band of << F.g2_band_range_hz >>~Hz. The noise comprised white sensor noise, a cortical background of spontaneous brain activity, and the field of distant room sources (the room field). We computed each array's noise covariance in closed form and treated it as known in the detectability measure (the true covariance),
\begin{equation}
\symbf{C} = \symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{n}} + \sigma^{2}\,\symbf{L}\symbf{A}\symbf{L}^{\top} + \symbf{E}\symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{e}}\symbf{E}^{\top}.
\label{eq:C}
\end{equation}
Here, $\symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{n}}$ is the diagonal covariance of white sensor noise. In the brain-noise term, $\symbf{L}$ is the lead-field matrix of << F.g2_n_background >> independent cortex-normal background dipoles on a << F.g2_bg_grid_mm >>-mm grid, and the diagonal matrix $\symbf{A}$ contains their Voronoi-cell areas. In the room-field term, $\symbf{E}$ contains the array's responses to << F.g2_room_terms|words >> external-field terms (<< F.meth_env_uniform_terms|words >> uniform and << F.meth_env_gradient_terms|words >> linear-gradient components), and $\symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{e}}$ is the covariance of their coefficients. The background is a cortically constrained form of the random-dipole model of spontaneous activity \citep{demunck1992}. Each background dipole has a moment variance of $\sigma^{2}$ times its area ($\sigma^{2}$ = << F.meth_bg_var_per_mm2_nam2 >>~nAm$^{2}$ per mm$^{2}$). As for the targets, we omitted cortex within << F.g2_source_skull_clearance_mm >>~mm of the inner skull from the background.

We fitted two quantities to the sample dataset: the room-field covariance $\symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{e}}$, to its empty-room recording, and $\sigma^{2}$, set so that the modeled brain noise of the median good gradiometer matched the measured level, << F.meth_bg_calibration_grad_ft_cm >>~fT/cm root-mean-square. Everything else, apart from both systems' datasheet sensor noise, is predicted rather than fitted. These predictions come from the same recording, so the checks below test the model within that recording, not in another subject or session.

We analyzed four noise conditions: sensor noise only; sensor plus brain noise, the primary condition; sensor, brain and room noise; and the room field projected out. To represent interference suppression, we derived the last condition from the sensor, brain and room-noise condition by removing the room field from every array with one projection. This removes the array's responses to the room-field terms after weighting each channel by its inverse sensor-noise variance (Eq.~(S1)). We designated this condition, the room field projected out, as the secondary condition before any simulation.

To check the model, we compared its Neuromag covariance with the measured covariance and recomputed Neuromag's detectability using the measured one (Section~S3). We band-passed the recording to << F.rev_cov_band_hz >>~Hz and excluded one bad channel. The measured covariance was the empirical covariance of << F.rev_cov_n_windows >> pre-stimulus windows (<< F.rev_cov_window_s >>~s; << F.rev_cov_n_baseline_samples >> samples), without regularization or projections. We took its difference from the empty-room covariance (<< F.rev_cov_n_empty_room_samples >> samples) as the measured brain noise. To remove the inflation in detectability from estimating covariance with finite data, we generated << F.rev_cov_n_surrogates >> phase-randomized surrogates of the whole recording, preserving its spectra and cross-spectra. We estimated their covariance from the same samples and divided each target's measured detectability by the resulting inflation (median factor << F.rev_cov_det_combined_bias_ratio_3dp >>).

Because the OPM covariance cannot be measured here, we computed the implied OPM/Neuromag ratio under five assumptions about how each system's real noise departs from the model. In scenario A, both systems share the model's error, leaving the modeled ratio unchanged. Scenario B scales the variances of both cortical backgrounds by $k$ = << F.rev_cov_k_ratio >>, the factor that matches the modeled magnetometer brain-noise variance to the measured one. The remaining scenarios replace all or part of Neuromag's modeled noise with measured noise. Scenarios C and D use Neuromag's measured covariance: the variance of the OPM background is scaled by $k$ in C, whereas the OPM retains its modeled covariance in D. Scenario E combines Neuromag's measured empty-room noise with its modeled brain noise.

In each sensitivity analysis, we changed one element of the adult model and recalibrated $\sigma^{2}$. We added cortex << F.rev_ns_joint_floor_mm >>--<< F.rev_ns_near_rule_mm >>~mm from the inner skull, termed the near-skull cortex, to the background, the targets, or both. We also tested colored OPM noise (1/$f$ plus white noise, with corners at << F.rev_ns_col_corners_hz >>~Hz) under a matched filter that also whitens across frequency. Separate analyses added idealized cardiac and ocular sources. The joint runs added the near-skull cortex to the background and gave the OPM noise a << F.rev_ns_joint_corner_hz >>-Hz corner under the frequency-whitening detector, with and without the cardiac and ocular sources. We did not model noise specific to OPMs, such as cross-talk between neighboring sensors and errors in gain and sensitive axis. The colored-noise variants alone represented the distance-independent low-frequency noise of a real OPM (Section~S3.2).

\subsection{Simulated interictal spikes}\label{sec:spikes}

We designed the spike simulations to ask whether the detectability difference carries over to transient-event detection by an idealized detector under the modeled noise. These simulations do not test the noise model. We ran the noise model in the time domain, using segments shared by all arrays, and stretched the spike-wave complex of \citet{hunold2016} to << F.wr_n_morphologies_words >> morphologies. We analyzed the data with two matched-filter detectors, both whitened using a Ledoit--Wolf covariance \citep{ledoit2004} estimated from << F.g4_null_baseline_minutes >>~min of null data, rather than with the true covariance.

The oracle detector knew the source and time and tested at a per-trial false-positive probability of << F.g4_oracle_alpha >>. The practical detector knew neither and scanned candidate field patterns on a << F.g4_dictionary_spacing_mm >>-mm grid that did not contain the true sources. We fixed its thresholds in advance (frozen) on independent null data for a nominal << F.g4_operating_point_per_minute >> false event per minute. Both detectors are idealized because they use the true forward model and the simulated waveform family. We interpolated S50, the dipole strength detected in << F.g4_s50_level >> of events, linearly in log strength between tested strengths. In both spike runs, the smaller heads were at top contact in the fixed adult helmet, whereas the adult was at its measured head position. At this position, the adult's area-weighted $D$ is << F.g3b_adult_centred_minus_top_d_abs >>~dB higher than at top contact.

In the exploratory run, we placed spikes at << F.g4_n_locations >> locations per anatomy, stratified by orientation and depth, with << F.g4_locations_per_band >> locations in each of << F.wr_n_depth_bands_words >> depth bands, ranging from << F.g4_band_10_20mm >> to << F.g4_band_45_70mm >>~mm. At each location, we simulated << F.g4_n_focal_events_per_location >> focal events of << F.g4_strength_range >>~nAm and patch events from cortical patches of << F.g4_patch_radius_mm >>-mm radius. Each event had its own noise realization and was identical for every array. All comparisons were within an anatomy; the anatomies are not ranked against one another. We also localized spikes at << F.loc_n_locations >> locations by equivalent-current-dipole fits and dynamic statistical parametric mapping (dSPM; \citealp{dale2000}) at the true peak sample. Localization used a single-compartment BEM and a coregistration error of << F.loc_coreg_shift_mm >>~mm and << F.loc_coreg_angle_deg >>°. Paired comparisons of localization error included every event, detected or not; a joint measure also required detection (Section~S7).

We then pre-specified a second run. Its endpoint compared the dense OPM array with Neuromag for focal spikes << F.g4_band_10_20mm >>~mm below the scalp in every anatomy, under the practical detector at a nominal << F.g4_operating_point_per_minute >> false event per minute. We drew << F.meth_confirm_locs >> new locations per anatomy and << F.wr_confirm_replicates_words >> noise replicates per event, with the first replicate carrying the endpoint. Separate null data were used for threshold fitting and rate evaluation. The threshold was set on << F.cf_null_calibration_minutes >>~min of calibration null data and rests on about << F.wr_confirm_calib_expected_events >> false events, corresponding to a Poisson uncertainty of about << F.wr_confirm_calib_poisson_pct >>. The oracle detector's threshold does not depend on this calibration.

The declared statistic was a two-sided sign-flip test on per-location differences in detection counts, Holm-adjusted over the anatomies \citep{holm1979} at $\alpha$ = << F.meth_alpha >>. The paired S50 ratio $r$ served as the effect size; a value of $r$ means that the OPM array detects a spike at a strength $100\,(1 - 1/r)$\,\% lower than Neuromag. The declared test estimated p by Monte Carlo when more than << F.meth_signflip_exact_max >> location differences were nonzero. We instead report exact p values over all sign patterns, which give the same decisions in all << F.cf_exact_n_families >> families of Holm-adjusted tests (Section~S7). As secondary analyses, we declared a mismatched detector using off-template morphologies, candidate patterns from a single-compartment BEM and a shared coregistration error of << F.wr_confirm_coreg_shift_mm >>~mm and << F.wr_confirm_coreg_angle_deg >>°. Further secondary analyses covered the site-matched array, the oracle detector and thresholds re-fitted on held-out null data.

\subsection{Statistical analysis}\label{sec:stats}

To preserve dependence between neighboring targets within a parcel, we computed intervals over targets as << F.g2_ci_level_pct >> percentile bootstrap intervals that resample atlas parcels as units: the adult's << F.g2_n_parcels >> labels or each smaller head's parcels. We used << F.meth_boot_g2 >> resamples for primary comparisons, noise-model sensitivity analyses and the measured-covariance check, and << F.meth_boot_g2_sens >> for the other adult sensitivity analyses and some secondary constructions of the smaller heads (Table~S16). For $\Delta$ and the interaction, the medians of paired differences between a smaller head and the adult, we resampled parcels together with their paired differences. In the spike study, locations were the units of resampling and testing.

Within one anatomy and noise model, the parcel intervals describe the sensitivity of a whole-cortex median to the parcels it pools. They are neither population confidence intervals nor measures of the spread of effects across the cortex, and they exclude the larger uncertainties of model error, head placement and between-subject variation. Whether an interval includes 1 (or 0~dB) is therefore reported descriptively, not as a test. Table~\ref{tab:estimands} lists the main estimands, and Table~S2 lists all of them.

Several choices were made after results were known. After the whole-cortex results were known, we named the primary helmet placement, the regions of interest and the exploratory spike endpoint, chosen from << F.g4_family_per_opm_array >> comparisons per OPM array. We later added the helmet fitted at the adult's gap, the measured-covariance check with its five scenarios and the sensitivity analyses; these additions did not change the model. The model itself, however, was revised while results were known (Section~S8.2).

We declared the pre-specified run in a configuration file committed to the public code repository the day before the run, which used the same code and configuration. Only the repository history attests this; the run was not registered externally. We chose the number of locations, double the exploratory << F.wr_confirm_pilot_locs_per_band >> per band, after << F.wr_confirm_pilot_runs_words >> pilot runs in child A. Their outputs were not retained, and their seeds were not reused.

