\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Determinants of the adult comparison}\label{sec:res-adult}

Brain noise reversed the comparison between the dense OPM array and Neuromag (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). With sensor noise alone, Neuromag had higher detectability than either OPM array (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}). When brain noise was added, the dense array had the higher detectability at << F.wr_g2_ahead_n >> of the << F.g2_n_targets >> cortical targets, with a median ratio to Neuromag of << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >> over all targets. The site-matched array, however, did not differ clearly from Neuromag (<< F.g2_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci >>). Projecting out the room field from every array gave the same pattern, although the site-matched array's ratio was then slightly below parity.

Brain noise dominated both systems' noise (Section~S3). With the room field included, brain noise accounted for << F.g2_noise_share_dense_brain_pct >> of an OPM channel's in-band variance in the dense array and << F.g2_noise_share_squid_mag_brain_pct >> of a Neuromag magnetometer's. The room field accounted for most of the remaining magnetometer variance (<< F.g2_noise_share_squid_mag_room_pct >>). Brain noise thus raised Neuromag magnetometer noise about << F.g2_noise_inflation_squid_mag_1dp >>-fold above its sensor floor, compared with only about << F.g2_noise_inflation_dense_1dp >>-fold for an OPM. The higher OPM sensor noise therefore mattered less once brain noise was present.

The Neuromag channels used as comparator and the SNR measure also affected the ratio (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). The dense array's advantage was larger against the gradiometers alone than against the magnetometers alone (Table~\ref{tab:adult}). Peak-channel SNR, the closest analog of sensor-level SNR in clinical studies (Section~S1), favored the dense array against the magnetometers, whereas it favored Neuromag when Neuromag's best channel of all << F.g2_squid_channels >> was used. Both OPM arrays had only small advantages in mean-power SNR (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}). Thus, which system a reported SNR favors depends on the Neuromag channels and SNR measure used.

Among the model variants, the OPM white-noise floor had the largest effect on the ratio (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). As it increased over the tested range, the dense-array ratio decreased to parity, whereas the site-matched ratio fell below it (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D). Under the modeled noise, the break-even OPM noise of the dense array, the OPM white noise at which its ratio equals 1, was << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_ib_asd >> << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_ib_asd_ci >>~fT/√Hz (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D, diamonds). The site-matched array broke even at << F.rev_ns_breakeven_matched_vs_combined_ib_asd >> << F.rev_ns_breakeven_matched_vs_combined_ib_asd_ci >>~fT/√Hz, an interval that includes the primary OPM noise level. With the room field projected out, the dense array's break-even level fell to << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_proj_asd >> << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_proj_asd_ci >>~fT/√Hz (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D, open diamonds), and the site-matched array's fell to << F.rev_ns_breakeven_matched_vs_combined_proj_asd >> << F.rev_ns_breakeven_matched_vs_combined_proj_asd_ci >>~fT/√Hz. All these levels assume the modeled Neuromag noise, which the next section compares with measured noise.

The other model variants changed the dense-array ratio less over the ranges tested (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). An additional OPM-to-scalp gap reduced the ratio without reversing it. Combining an OPM noise of << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >>~fT/√Hz with an additional << F.g2_opm_gap_small_mm >>-mm gap did reverse it (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}), giving a ratio of << F.g2_joint_gap3mm_asd30ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_joint_gap3mm_asd30ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>. The triaxial array at the site-matched sites recovered most of the dense array's advantage, and still recovered part of it when its tangential axes were twice as noisy (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}, triangles). The ratio changed little with a covariance estimated from as many simulated samples as a minute of data would contain, or with a 3--70-Hz band instead of the primary band. Thus, besides brain noise, array design and the OPM noise floor determined which system had the higher detectability in the adult; the comparator and SNR measure determined which system a reported SNR favored.

