\subsection{The noise model against measured Neuromag noise}\label{sec:res-noise}

The noise model reproduced some properties of Neuromag's measured noise and missed others (Figs.~S2 and S3). It predicted << F.rev_cov_brain_mag_amp_ratio >> of the measured magnetometer brain-noise amplitude, which was not used in calibration, and << F.rev_cov_heart_mag_amp_without_ratio >> with the heart's field removed. Calibrating the brain-noise scale on a random half of the sites gave agreement with the other half's measured gradiometer level nearly by construction (<< F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ratio >>; Fig.~S3B). Across << F.rev_cov_heldout_n_splits >> random splits, the << F.g2_ci_level_pct >> range of this model-to-measured ratio was << F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ci >>. However, the modeled brain noise was too flat from inferior to superior sites. The model calibrated on the inferior half predicted << F.rev_cov_heldout_inf_to_sup_grad_amp_ratio >> times the measured gradiometer amplitude in the superior half; calibrated on the superior half, it predicted << F.rev_cov_heldout_sup_to_inf_grad_amp_ratio >> times that in the inferior half. The model also predicted per-channel variances poorly, and its magnetometer correlations decreased with distance faster than measured correlations. Thus, the measured covariance differed from the modeled one in structure as well as in level.

The measured brain noise also contained variance that the model lacks. This excess, the positive part of the difference between the measured and modeled magnetometer brain-noise covariances, summed to << F.rev_cov_excess_brain_mag_pos_over_model_ratio >> times the summed modeled brain-noise variance. Sampling error alone would produce an excess of << F.rev_cov_excess_brain_mag_pos_over_model_null_range >> times that variance. Nevertheless, Neuromag's detectability was higher with its measured covariance than with its modeled covariance (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}A). The median ratio of these two Neuromag detectabilities (measured over modeled covariance) was << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_ci >>, as large as the dense array's entire advantage. It varied by lobe, from << F.rev_cov_det_lobe_temporal_corrected_ratio >> in the temporal lobe to << F.rev_cov_det_lobe_cingulate_corrected_ratio >> in the cingulate. This higher detectability may appear paradoxical, but detectability depends on the structure of the covariance, not on its total variance alone. Despite greater channel-level noise, the measured covariance gave higher detectability at << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_share >> of targets, so it cannot exceed the modeled covariance in every spatial direction. Its excess lay in a few far-field components that whitening down-weights (Section~S3).

What the measured Neuromag noise implies for the OPM arrays depends on the real OPM noise, which no measurement here constrains (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}B; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). Both arrays' ratios were nearly unchanged under shared model error (scenario A) or with the variances of both cortical backgrounds scaled to match the measured magnetometer brain noise (scenario B). Using Neuromag's measured empty-room noise with modeled brain noise also left the ratios nearly unchanged (scenario E). Scenario C was least favorable to OPMs: Neuromag's measured covariance and the OPM background scaled as in scenario B lowered both arrays' ratios below parity. With Neuromag's measured covariance and OPM noise unchanged from the model (scenario D), the dense-array ratio decreased to << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ci >>, an interval that includes 1 (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}B). The site-matched ratio fell below parity, to << F.rev_cov_opm_matched_vs_combined_s4_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_opm_matched_vs_combined_s4_ci >>. Thus, under scenario D, neither array had a clear advantage over Neuromag at the primary OPM noise level.

We did not compute break-even levels under scenario D, but their direction follows from these ratios. Neuromag's measured detectability is independent of OPM noise, so the scenario-D ratio, like the modeled ratio, falls as OPM noise rises. At the point estimate of << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ratio >> at << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz, the dense array would break even above that noise level, although its ratio interval includes 1. The site-matched array, with a ratio of << F.rev_cov_opm_matched_vs_combined_s4_ratio >>, would break even below that level, if at all within the tested range. The medians alone cannot determine how far either level would move from its modeled value, because the measured-noise correction varies across targets.

The tested model omissions changed the dense array's advantage little at the primary OPM noise (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}C; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). Adding the near-skull cortex as background or as additional targets changed the ratio little (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}C). Colored OPM noise at the highest tested corner frequency, << F.rev_ns_joint_corner_hz >>~Hz, slightly reduced the ratio under both the primary and frequency-whitening detectors. Adding cardiac and ocular sources, separately or together, likewise changed the ratio by only << F.rev_ns_ff_dense_vs_combined_ib_change_pct_range >>.

The joint runs combined the near-skull background and a << F.rev_ns_joint_corner_hz >>-Hz OPM-noise corner under the frequency-whitening detector (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D, dash-dotted). With white OPM noise at << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz, this detector gave a dense-array ratio of << F.rev_ns_col_fr_asd15ft_c0hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.rev_ns_col_fr_asd15ft_c0hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >> (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}C). The joint run reduced it to << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >> (Table~\ref{tab:adult}). Raising white OPM noise alone to << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz reduced it more, to << F.rev_ns_joint_white_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.rev_ns_joint_white_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>. With both changes, the ratio was close to parity at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz (<< F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >>, or << F.rev_ns_joint_all_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.rev_ns_joint_all_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >> with the cardiac and ocular sources added) and below it at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >>~fT/√Hz (<< F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd30ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>; Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D). Under the same detector, the omitted noise sources thus lowered the ratio less than raising the OPM noise from << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >> to << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz did.

