Head placement in the helmet, an uncertainty that the parcel-bootstrap intervals do not include, mattered as much as head size. Across the fixed helmet's source-blind placements, the school-age scaled adult's change from the adult spanned << F.rev_cgap_school_band_dense_vs_combined_ib_range >>~dB (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}B). This range of differences of medians is wider than the parcel interval of the change at top contact, << F.g3b_school_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >>~dB. The change remained positive at every placement in each of the << F.wr_n_principal_words >> principal heads (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}B, gray ranges). Across the same placements, the adult's own $D$ spanned << F.g3b_adult_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >>~dB. The << F.wr_n_provisional_words >> heads reported separately in Section~S10 had a fixed-helmet $\Delta$ of << F.g3b_provisional_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB, with the smallest changes in the << F.wr_n_children_words >> school-aged children (<< F.g3b_children_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB). A larger OPM advantage in a smaller head may therefore reflect adult-helmet fit as much as the head itself.

\subsection{Helmet fit versus head size}\label{sec:res-fit}

In every principal smaller head, the fixed adult helmet was less favorable to Neuromag relative to the fitted helmet than it was in the adult (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}C; Table~\ref{tab:heads}). The interaction ranged from << F.wr_cgap_principal_interaction_range >>~dB, with every interval above zero. The scaled adults defined the extremes, with the templates intermediate at << F.wr_cgap_principal_templates_interaction_range >>~dB. In the << F.wr_n_provisional_words >> heads reported separately in Section~S10, the interaction was << F.wr_cgap_provisional_interaction_range >>~dB; only child C's interval included zero (<< F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_combined_ib_interaction >> << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_combined_ib_interaction_ci >>~dB).

Part of the interaction comes from the adult, whose contrast between the helmets was negative (<< F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib >>~dB). This negative contrast is consistent with the adult's larger median gap in the fitted helmet (<< F.rev_cgap_adult_gap_mm >>~mm) than at top contact (<< F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_gap_mm >>~mm). The negative adult contrast is the main reason the interaction exceeded each principal head's within-head contrast (<< F.rev_cgap_principal_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_range >>~dB). About half of the adult contrast, however, arises from constructing the fitted helmet itself. Between top contact and a helmet fitted at the adult's top-contact gap, which have the same median gap, the contrast was << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib >> << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib_ci >>~dB (Section~S5).

