With each head in a fitted helmet and with its own dense array, most of the smaller heads' extra OPM advantage disappeared (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}B; Table~\ref{tab:heads}). When both the smaller head's helmet and the adult's were fitted at the adult's gap, the change $\Delta$ was << F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB, with interval bounds of << F.wr_cgap_principal_fitted_delta_ci_bounds_range >>~dB (Table~\ref{tab:heads}). Every interval included zero except the school-age scaled adult's, whose lower bound was just above zero. Fitting instead at the adult's top-contact gap left a change of << F.wr_cgap_principal_fittedtop_vs_top_range >>~dB relative to the adult at top contact (Section~S5). With the room field projected out, the fitted-helmet change was negative in every principal head (<< F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_range >>~dB); only << F.wr_cgap_principal_fitted_proj_delta_n_ci_includes_zero_words >> of the << F.wr_n_principal_words >> intervals included zero (Table~S6). Thus, the fitted-helmet change from the adult was a few hundredths of a decibel and became negative after projecting out the room field.

The fitted helmet changes the sensor gap, placement and coverage together, so none of the quantities reported here apportions the effects of these factors exactly. At equal OPM site count, the fitted-helmet change increased to << F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB (Table~\ref{tab:heads}), compared with << F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB with each head's own array and << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB in the fixed helmet. Fitting therefore removed almost all the extra advantage only because each smaller head had its own, smaller dense array. The comparison suggests that, in the fitted helmet, the scaled adults and templates retained a gain of a few tenths of a decibel from their smaller size, a gain that their fewer OPM sites offset.

\subsection{Simulated interictal spikes}\label{sec:res-spikes}

Under the modeled noise, idealized detectors at a nominal false-event rate detected simulated superficial spikes at lower strength with the dense array than with Neuromag in the adult and every principal smaller head (Fig.~\ref{fig:confirm}A; Table~\ref{tab:spikes}). The pre-specified run's declared endpoint, the S50 ratio Neuromag/OPM, was << F.cf_adult_principal_dense_vs_combined_practical_ratio_range >> in these anatomies, with every location-bootstrap interval above 1 (Table~\ref{tab:spikes}). For spikes << F.cf_band >>~mm deep, the dense array thus detected << F.g4_s50_level >> of events at a << F.cf_adult_principal_dense_vs_combined_practical_reduction_pct_range >> lower dipole strength. The smaller heads were at top contact in the fixed adult helmet, whose fit raises their modeled OPM advantage (Section~\ref{sec:res-fit}). Their detection advantage therefore includes an effect of helmet fit; spikes were not simulated in the fitted helmet. As expected with higher detectability, the declared sign-flip test favored the dense array in each of these anatomies (p << F.cf_adult_principal_dense_vs_combined_practical_p_holm_max|replace("<", "< ") >> in each of these << F.cf_adult_principal_n_anatomies|words >>, Holm-adjusted over the << F.meth_n_anatomies|words >> declared anatomies; Table~S11). In the adult, the S50 ratio (<< F.cf_adult_dense_vs_combined_practical_ratio >>) was of the order of the detectability ratio at these depths (<< F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_15_20_ratio >>--<< F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_10_15_ratio >>; Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}A), so the detectability advantage carried over to the detection of these spikes.

