\section{Conclusions}\label{sec:conclusions}

We compared on-scalp OPM arrays with a cryogenic Neuromag system in simulations with explicit sensor, brain and room noise. In the adult, the array design and the OPM noise floor determined which system had the higher detectability. The comparator and the SNR measure determined which system a reported SNR favored. The dense single-axis array's modeled advantage was as large as the model's error for Neuromag noise. Its break-even OPM noise of << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_ib_asd >>~fT/√Hz therefore applies only under modeled Neuromag noise; with measured Neuromag noise, the dense array had no clear advantage at << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz. The single-axis site-matched array showed no clear difference from Neuromag, whereas three equally quiet axes at those sites approached the dense array; clinical dual- and triaxial arrays were not modeled. With each smaller head's own dense array, fitting the SQUID helmet removed most of the extra OPM advantage produced by the adult helmet. Under the modeled noise, idealized detectors detected weaker superficial spikes with the dense array than with Neuromag at a nominal false-event rate. Measured OPM covariances would test the adult comparison, and comparisons in children depend on how well the SQUID helmet fits.

